/A 2464 0 R
/Alt (The two-panel map shows relative risk across European cities. Panel a, labeled Daytime, displays a map of Europe with various regions outlined. A legend on the right indicates relative risk values from 1.00 to 1.10 \(lightest shade\) up to greater than 2.00 \(darkest shade\). Regions with higher relative risk are concentrated in Scandinavia, Eastern Europe, and parts of Southern Europe. Insets show magnified views of England, Poland, and Belgium and the Netherlands. Cities like Innlandet, Stockholm, Kurzeme, Klaipeda, London, Walbrzych, Koimbla, Barcelona, Rome, and Athens are labeled. Panel b, labeled Nighttime, shows a similar map of Europe with the same legend. The distribution of relative risk values appears visually similar to panel a, with some regional variations in intensity. Both panels show a general trend of higher relative risk in specific urban and surrounding areas, with the darkest shades indicating risk greater than 2.00.)
